{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792696217,"nonce":"pi21BddO8DQTeg8+TuHlToHzGgQ=","message":"Native primitive path-clipping implementation and focused verification\n\nScope: SDF primitive emission, solid/gradient fills and strokes routed through PRIM_MESH, shadow primitives and canvas text converted to PRIM_TEXT. The existing shader shape evaluator and GPU primitive layout are reused. This is a child of 732c3370857424d2e3c7ccfcde44fe0095471eda74dae4e5b415daca036738fe, which stays open for the separate native tessellated-path brush fallback, glass/backdrops and independently prepared text/glyph streams.\n\nImplementation:\n- PathClipData still lazily prepares push-time physical contours and fill rules with the existing flattening/invalid-input limits. Native consumers copy only its three-row shape header and point rows, cached once per active batch. Static, dynamic and composite drains invalidate those offsets. Foreground projection retains the original aux pool.\n- Primitive emission attaches the existing one-based clip_shape pointer and preserves the rectangular scissor separately. Topmost nonrectangular selection and scratch ancestor stripping are unchanged. Mesh emission resolves the clip once before its triangle loop, avoiding contour copies or stack scans per triangle.\n- GpuPrimitive::rebase_aux handles header/table, mesh, polygon and 3D-group references. Native batch merges and canvas concatenation use it. The 3D-group selector is explicit so ordinary per-side border widths are not shifted as pointers.\n- Legacy tight layer rendering translates each referenced inverse clip frame once, even when many primitives share it, and restores the original aux pool. The ordered scratch renderer already handled this frame format.\n- Primitive-only redraw_canvases cannot replace referenced rows, so auxiliary-backed replays fall back to a full paint. This is a correctness fallback, not in-place contour cache patching; it can cost more paint work for these canvases. The compositor's separate canvas collection continues to emit fresh batches. No timing benchmark was run.\n\nNegative controls:\n- Before wiring: the first two app controls both fail. Direct clipped rectangles paint at (10,10) outside the path; tight rendering paints (13,13) outside transformed rings. Log: /private/tmp/blinc-native-path-negative.log.\n- Initial wiring separately exposed missing legacy tight-frame translation at (62,20), and non-compositor canvas replay retaining stale rows at (110,70) on frame 1. Both cases are now covered by the passing controls.\n- The border merge regression fails with [3,7,18,5] instead of [3,7,11,5] when seven prefix rows are present, proving the inherited positive-border heuristic corrupts widths. The explicit group selector fixes it. Log: /private/tmp/blinc-native-path-border-negative.log.\n- The first draft of the app test needed a Gradient constructor and borrow-lifetime correction before it compiled. The original outer-shadow opacity-group reference produced no meaningful painted samples because the legacy group bounds cut off its halo; the final control checks inner shadows in groups and outer shadows without a group. No shadow-halo redesign is claimed.\n\nFocused final checks, CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2, primitive/glyph capacities 1024, BLINC_MULTISTOP_VERTEX=1:\n- In native SSBO, forced VB and forced DT modes: cargo test -p blinc_app native_path_clip --lib -- --include-ignored --test-threads=1. Six pass in each mode: five GPU cases plus one CPU scratch-hash case. Pixels cover seven draw kinds, static/motion/scratch owners, both streams, rectangular scissors, opacity groups, push-time affine/DPI composition, nonzero/even-odd rings, ancestor stripping, tight origins/shared frames, invalid/empty/singular clips, merged headers, legacy overlays, ordered overlays at 1x/4x MSAA, two canvases and changed/empty frames through all three application entry points.\n- In each mode: the existing rgba_path_clip_curves_and_scissors_survive_every_owner_and_stream app GPU case passes, covering shared path preparation with raw images; cargo test -p blinc_test_suite aux_routing --lib -- --ignored --test-threads=1 passes both relevant routing GPU regressions.\n- Normal mode CPU integration filters: native_path_clip (4), rgba_path_clip (4), aux_routing (4), foreground_layers_projection_moves_paths_and_merge_rebases_cursors (1). All pass. The old raw-path fallback assertion is updated to the newly supported native primitive behavior.\n- Driver: /private/tmp/blinc_native_path_scoped_modes.py. Logs: /private/tmp/blinc-native-path-{normal,vb,dt}-*.log. Temporary capability overrides are restored after the driver.\n- Touched Rust files are formatted with rustfmt --edition 2024 --config skip_children=true. No workspace-wide, DSL, docs, platform or unrelated test/lint gates. No browser/mobile pixel runs or performance benchmarks.\n\nStructural memory: native batch payloads use 3 + ceil(prepared_point_count/2) vec4 rows, shared by all primitives using that path in the owning batch. Existing raw-image headers remain separate. Empty/invalid clips use three rows with count zero. Ordinary non-path emission adds no contour allocation. GPU fields and shaders are unchanged. These are structural sizes, not allocator or timing measurements.","files":null}]}